<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Flushing, Queens</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1645">1645</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Vlissingen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Flushing is a neighbourhood in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> City borough of Queens, founded under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> rule in 1645 as the English settlement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlissingen">Vlissingen</a>. In 1657 its residents signed the Flushing Remonstrance, protesting against Director-General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-stuyvesant">Peter Stuyvesant</a>'s persecution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quakers">Quakers</a>, an early American statement of religious freedom. It is now home to one of the largest Chinese and Korean communities in New York.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/flushing-queens.jpg" alt="Flushing, Queens" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Raman Patel · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flushing,_Queens,_NY,_USA_-_panoramio_(1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Flushing lies in the north-central part of the New York City borough of Queens, on the eastern bank of Flushing Creek, now the Flushing River. Before European settlement the area was inhabited by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lenape">Lenape</a>. Downtown Flushing is a major commercial and retail area, and the intersection of Main Street and Roosevelt Avenue at its core is among the busiest in the city. The neighbourhood is served by stations on the Long Island Rail Road's Port Washington Branch and by the IRT Flushing Line of the subway, which ends at Main Street.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The settlement was established on 10 October 1645 under a charter of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-west-india-company">Dutch West India Company</a>, as part of the colony of New Netherland governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-amsterdam">New Amsterdam</a>. It was named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlissingen">Vlissingen</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> city that served as the company's European base, and the English form Flushing was eventually settled upon.</p><p>Many early settlers had come down from the Connecticut Colony. After the English took control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-amsterdam">New Amsterdam</a> in 1664, the Town of Flushing became one of the five original towns of Queens County when it was established in 1683. The town was dissolved in 1898, when Queens became a borough of the City of Greater New York.</p><p>In the 19th century Flushing's closeness to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manhattan">Manhattan</a> made it a fashionable residential area, and the Village of Flushing was incorporated on 15 April 1837. The Queensboro Bridge, opened in 1909 to midtown Manhattan, and the extension of the Port Washington Branch to Pennsylvania Station the next year brought development.</p><p>The Roosevelt Avenue Bridge, carrying the Flushing Line, was the largest trunnion bascule bridge in the world when it was finished in 1927, and the subway terminal on Main Street opened the following year. In the 1970s immigrants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> settled in what had been a mostly white neighbourhood with a small Japanese community, and a large Korean population also made Flushing its home. Later arrivals came from many provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and other countries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Flushing Chinatown, centred on Main Street and Roosevelt Avenue, has more than 30,000 residents born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and is described as the largest Chinatown outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. A Koreatown that began on Union Street has spread east along Northern Boulevard into Murray Hill, Bayside, Douglaston and Little Neck. The neighbourhood includes residential sections such as Broadway-Flushing, whose elegant park-like streets form a state and federal historic district, and Waldheim, an estate subdivision built mainly between 1875 and 1925.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Flushing, Queens” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The rule prevailed twice against Stuyvesant's narrow outlook, in the grant of full residency to Ashkenazi and Sephardi Jews in New Amsterdam in 1655 and in the <b>Flushing</b> Remonstrance of 1657, when some thirty residents petitioned against his ban on Quaker worship.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-netherland">New Netherland · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Some thirty residents of <b>Flushing</b> in New Netherland, none of them Quakers, signed a petition asking Director-General Peter Stuyvesant to exempt them from his ban on Quaker worship.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-27">27 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
